From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 21:59:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Add the shview-style response viewer and the
 deconvolution Monte Carlo

Two pieces of work.

1. Viewing the SMI kernel as a response function.

SMI has a kernel, CSD has a response function, and they describe the same
object: for a single fibre, R(theta) = sum_l K_l(b) (2l+1) P_l(cos theta), so
the zonal harmonic coefficients are r_l = K_l(b)*sqrt((2l+1)*4*pi), which is
exactly what MRtrix keeps in a response file.

- SMI_response_helpers.m computes the zonal coefficients from a kernel, builds
  shview-style glyphs for both responses and fODFs, and reads and writes MRtrix
  response text files.
- example_SMI_response_shview.m draws it four ways per shell, takes the
  compartments apart, puts a response glyph next to an fODF glyph, and writes
  response_SMI_wm.txt for `shview`. It self-checks the zonal reconstruction
  against SMI's own forward model before drawing anything (1.7e-15).
- test_SMI_response_helpers.m: 8 self-contained tests, all passing under
  Octave.

2. deconv_comparison/ -- constrained SMI vs MSMT-CSD vs SSST-CSD.

Monte Carlo in the design of Jeurissen et al. (2014): 10,000 Rician
realisations per condition, crossings at 15/45/60 degrees plus a single fibre,
SNR 5 to noise free, response functions from a reimplementation of
`dwi2response dhollander` (with `tournier` and `fa` alongside), one peak finder
with sub-vertex refinement applied to all four arms.

The two SMI arms share one kernel fit, so the constrained and unconstrained
numbers differ by the deconvolution alone.

Report and result tables in REPORT_SMI_deconvolution_MonteCarlo.md. Everything
is simulation, including the data the responses are estimated from.
