From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 22:39:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Complete the Monte Carlo comparison: 10,000 realisations
 per condition

Five SNR arms (50, 30, 20, 10, 5) plus a noise-free reference, four
deconvolutions each, 40,000 voxels per arm. Result tables in deconv_tables.md,
figure in fodf_deconv_montecarlo.png, discussion in
REPORT_SMI_deconvolution_MonteCarlo.md section 5.

The four methods split into two behaviours, and the split is a bias-variance
trade rather than a ranking.

Constrained SMI and MSMT-CSD are almost insensitive to noise -- constrained
SMI's angular correlation on a 15 degree crossing is 0.9469 noise free and
0.9411 at SNR 5 -- and both are biased: neither resolves a 45 degree crossing
at any SNR, including noise free, and both sit ~22 degrees off there because
the largest peak of a single merged lobe is what gets measured.

The unconstrained deconvolution of the same kernel is essentially exact and
essentially unusable: ACC 0.9998 noise free, 45 degrees resolved in 100% of
realisations, and 2.757 spurious peaks per single-fibre voxel at SNR 5.

SSST-CSD sits between them everywhere. Its 45 degree peaks separate but land
~11 degrees out, because the estimated response is blunt; with an exact
response that drops to 4.13 degrees and the resolution rate goes from 27.5% to
83.8%.

The crossover is around SNR 10-20. Below it constrained SMI is the most
faithful arm and the only one still returning the correct fibre count in 87% of
60 degree crossings at SNR 5, where SSST-CSD manages 17% and the unconstrained
deconvolution 0.1%.

Also here: run_csd_par.py (the MSMT-CSD fits split across processes, byte
identical output), the exact-response control, and the figure/axis fixes.

